00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:19.740
from Major League Baseball.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:20.560
KRIS AMY: Hi, Amr.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.350
Thanks for having me on today.

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.130
I run the team at
MLB that covers off

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:27.160
all the infrastructure for
our fan-facing services

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.790
which run on Anthos
and the Google Cloud.

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:32.490
I look forward to showing
you what we've got to offer.

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:33.490
AMR ABDELRAZIK: Awesome.

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:34.670
Thank you, Kris.

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:38.490
So the first question
is, why the edge and why

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:39.740
are we talking about the edge?

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:43.900
So customers are demanding
a more connected experience,

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.410
and this is happening
across different sectors,

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:49.230
whether it is retail,
connected factories, health

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:50.860
care, and even gaming.

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:55.390
What this means is enterprises
need to deploy applications

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:58.000
in many places,
whether from the left

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.270
you're looking at something
at the very far edge, hundreds

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:05.175
of thousands of sites, very low
latency, very small footprint.

00:01:05.175 --> 00:01:06.550
And some of the
use cases of that

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:09.830
is real time control,
industrial security real time.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.850
Or you get something
like the near edge, where

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:15.160
you have less
sites, probably less

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:16.887
sensitivity on the
latency, and you're

00:01:16.887 --> 00:01:18.970
looking at something like
video and image analysis

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:20.100
and processing.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.480
Or you go more to looking at
the traditional data center

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:23.900
core or cloud.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:27.550
And the challenge is, how do
you deploy across all of these?

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:29.440
And this is hard,
because as you're

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.230
looking to deploy across
all of these applications,

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:34.840
you're looking at
migrating applications.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:38.830
The developers are deploying
applications differently.

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:41.260
Every cloud or every
site is different.

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:44.740
You need to deploy the
application multiple times.

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:46.630
You can't move everything
to every location,

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:50.320
so it is just hard to
try to manage across all

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.030
of these infrastructure.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:53.710
And the good news
is there is actually

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:55.430
a perfect storm of
opportunity about some

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:57.430
of the technologies
happening in the market that

00:01:57.430 --> 00:01:59.690
have been happening over
the last couple of months.

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:02.520
So the first one, of
course, is 5G, but also

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.690
a lot of connectivity
options that are out there

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:07.990
that is improving connectivity,
latency, and bandwidth

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:09.580
for those edge scenarios.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:11.410
The second one is
containers, when

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:12.940
you have a portable
application form

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:16.270
factor that allows you to deploy
across different environments

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:17.260
with ease.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:19.380
And finally, there
is the open source,

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.030
the continuous innovation
with no vendor lock-in,

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.430
led by Kubernetes and
many other technologies

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:26.180
that are out there
in the markets.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:28.840
So this presents a
great opportunity

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:33.430
to use all these new innovations
and leverage your existing data

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:36.650
and deliver your applications
to where you want.

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:39.280
So we [? have ?] a little bit
of a how can Anthos help you

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:40.420
with this problem.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:43.910
So Anthos is our platform for
application modernization.

00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:46.210
It allows you to
run on Google Cloud,

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:49.180
on premise, on other cloud
providers, or at the edge.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:53.110
And the idea is Anthos gives you
managed operation, the service

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:56.020
management part, and the
marketplace and commerce

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:58.690
so that you can even add more
applications from third party

00:02:58.690 --> 00:02:59.825
providers.

00:02:59.825 --> 00:03:03.130
And Anthos gives you
multiple deployment options

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:07.510
from cloud platform, as we
discussed, vSphere, AWS.

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:10.060
You can even bring
your own Kubernetes,

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:13.710
or you can deploy it on bare
metal edge scenarios and Azure

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:15.100
that is coming soon.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.590
So across all of these options,
Anthos [? provide ?] you

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:21.220
the deployment flexibility to
deploy application anywhere

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:24.460
and have the consistency of
your operational workflow

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:26.337
and your developer workflow.

00:03:26.337 --> 00:03:28.170
So we're going to talk
a little about Anthos

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:31.090
on bare metal, a
technical overview, which

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:34.120
is our newest offering
that we announced recently.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:36.310
So the idea for
Anthos on bare metal

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:39.010
is you can run Anthos on a
customer-provided operating

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:40.130
system.

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:42.700
In this scenario, you
can install Anthos

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:45.605
where the customer deploys
and manages the OS lifecycle.

00:03:45.605 --> 00:03:47.980
You have pre-flight checks to
validate the minimum system

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:50.950
requirements, and then you
have idempotent and declarative

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:52.930
installer for all
Anthos components.

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:55.210
[? While ?] you can run it
from a compute capacity,

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:56.770
you can deploy
this on a physical

00:03:56.770 --> 00:03:58.285
or a virtual infrastructure.

00:03:58.285 --> 00:04:00.040
Now we expect most
of our customers

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:02.100
to run this on physical
server, but running

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.300
on your choice of
[INAUDIBLE] infrastructure

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:06.070
is also possible.

00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:09.250
We intend to support
RHEL, CentOS, and Ubuntu.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:11.230
And from a networking
standpoint,

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:14.140
N3 connectivity between
the nodes is required,

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:16.420
and we need L2 between
the load balancing nodes,

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:19.570
but Google provides an overlay
networking L4 and L7 node

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:20.620
balancing.

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:24.190
From a storage, you can
integrate with your storage

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:26.290
infrastructure using CSI.

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:29.230
Upgrade will be in place,
and from an observability

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:31.150
standpoint, of course
we have out of the box

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:33.790
Stackdriver [INAUDIBLE] Cloud
Monitoring metrics and logs.

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:36.580
Or you can integrate with the
customer-provided observability

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:37.640
if you want.

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:40.270
We're also considering
VM lifecycle options

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:43.010
with technologies like
KubeVirt and many others.

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:44.770
So this provides
you a very flexible

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:48.070
stack where you can deploy on
your existing infrastructure

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:52.370
and easily run
application using Anthos.

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:55.060
So in terms of managing
Anthos at the edge,

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:58.660
some of the technologies and
the capabilities that we have

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:00.830
makes it very easy to do so.

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:02.720
So we're going to
cover some of those.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:05.680
So the first one is the
standardized architecture.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.190
Again, you have the flexibility
to deploy on VMware,

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:12.020
deploy on your favorite
operating system.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:14.680
So this gives you
tremendous flexibility

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:18.490
to deploy where you are and not
have to retrofit your existing

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:19.850
infrastructure.

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:22.390
In fact, you can leverage
your existing infrastructure

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:25.750
to meet your current
needs, and you can even

00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:28.730
modernize it in place
without impacting the apps.

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:32.940
So that's the first piece of
the application modernization

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:35.800
managing [? of ?] [? it. ?] The
second is managing the entire

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:38.560
fleet, and one of the
challenges that I've seen a lot

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:41.230
of customers is they
have thousands of stores,

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:42.930
thousands of locations.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:46.180
So managing across all of
these locations is very hard,

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:48.940
and having a cloud-based
monitoring solution

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:51.100
and management solution
helps you there.

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:54.310
So one of the technologies
is the single pane of glass

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:57.010
to have for your
clusters and workloads.

00:05:57.010 --> 00:05:59.500
You can manage the cluster
from a single cloud-based web

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:00.490
console.

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:03.040
You have visibility
for your entire fleet,

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:04.450
no matter where they are.

00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:07.300
And you can deploy workflows
from a private registry

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:10.900
or from a GCP marketplace,
all of this from a console

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:13.810
or a centralized API
through Google Cloud.

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:16.870
So this gives you great
flexibility and a single point

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:19.160
to manage and deploy
all your cluster.

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:21.730
The second part is centralized
logging and monitoring.

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:24.720
As you look across all
of these locations,

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:26.710
you need to understand
what's up, what's not.

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:28.090
You need to diagnose.

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:30.160
Sending a technician
can sometimes

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:32.793
be very costly and
very time consuming,

00:06:32.793 --> 00:06:34.210
and have an impact
to the business

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:36.123
if you have a
store that is down.

00:06:36.123 --> 00:06:37.540
Centralized logging
and monitoring

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:41.440
helps you with that by
aligning all of the data

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:44.870
and pushing it to Stackdriver,
or now Cloud Monitoring.

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:46.510
And then you can see
what's happening,

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:49.450
error rates, performance.

00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:53.080
You can even manage the
lifecycle of those locations

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:58.420
and define SLO and monitoring
for your SRE codes and signals.

00:06:58.420 --> 00:07:01.810
So that's from operations,
and then that gives you

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:04.510
the fleet-wide policy
management, again through--

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:06.430
do this from a kubectl.

00:07:06.430 --> 00:07:08.620
You can also use GitOps.

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:13.040
So from a programmatic
SRE kind of standpoint,

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:14.680
you can use DevOps technologies.

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:16.900
You can have your
single team deploy,

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:20.290
for example, a canonical
version of the app, one place,

00:07:20.290 --> 00:07:23.860
and it gets replicated
across all of your stores

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:25.308
in a matter of seconds.

00:07:25.308 --> 00:07:27.100
So that also gives you
a lot of flexibility

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:31.130
of how to deploy apps
across the fleet.

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:34.300
So we want to talk about
edge deployment models here.

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:37.310
And one of the
interesting innovations

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:38.900
that we are introducing
to the market

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:43.970
as Google is, as a customer, you
don't have to manage or deploy

00:07:43.970 --> 00:07:44.560
all the apps.

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:47.400
We give you different
options to do so.

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:50.540
So the first part
worth mentioning

00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:52.140
is Google's global network.

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:55.220
And this is something that is
very unique to Google, not just

00:07:55.220 --> 00:07:58.520
by our data centers
but our connectivity

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:01.030
to region, PoPs, and
networks that you've

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:04.310
used over the years
for Search, for YouTube

00:08:04.310 --> 00:08:05.970
for Stadia and gaming.

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:09.860
So these are a combination
of CDN edge point presence,

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:13.910
dedicated interconnect, and even
cloud regions that gives you

00:08:13.910 --> 00:08:15.650
a lot of flexibility on that.

00:08:15.650 --> 00:08:17.810
So from that, we
have 24 regions.

00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:19.955
We have 144 edge
PoPs and thousands

00:08:19.955 --> 00:08:22.743
of edge nodes in
telecom networks.

00:08:22.743 --> 00:08:24.410
But that's the part
that we are starting

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:25.970
to have innovation for it.

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:28.790
As you integrate with
existing telecom partners,

00:08:28.790 --> 00:08:32.510
that gives you a lot of options
to expand our network even

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:36.980
further in this partnership and
gives you even closer points

00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:39.390
of presence across the globe.

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:43.559
So this gives us a flexible
operation model for the edge.

00:08:43.559 --> 00:08:46.130
So we have three
options that will

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:49.800
make your life a lot easier as
a customer to deploy the edge.

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:51.920
The first one is a
Google-managed edge.

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:55.580
In this case, you are
leveraging Google's capability,

00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:59.200
Google's infrastructure, for
example like edge caching

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:02.540
and service nodes and using
edge point of presence

00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:05.810
on Google Cloud, either core
regions and zones or edge

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:07.260
regions and zones.

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:08.730
So that's the first option.

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:12.230
The second option is telco
edge, leveraging our existing

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:16.170
partnership with
the telcos and using

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:18.090
their small central
office presence,

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:21.180
a large central office presence
to deploy this, for example.

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:23.800
So you don't even have
to have it on the store.

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:27.060
You can have using
a combination of 5G

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:31.390
and then have your equipment, or
actually the telco's equipment

00:09:31.390 --> 00:09:33.370
running your application.

00:09:33.370 --> 00:09:36.180
This gives you the ability
to deploy, for example,

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:37.580
next to your store.

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:40.360
So you don't have to have the
presence in the store itself,

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:41.818
but actually have
something that is

00:09:41.818 --> 00:09:43.175
very close and very connected.

00:09:43.175 --> 00:09:44.675
And finally, of
course, the customer

00:09:44.675 --> 00:09:47.270
edge, the classical
customer edge scenario,

00:09:47.270 --> 00:09:49.500
where you are deploying
on your existing branch

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:52.170
office or your existing
retail location.

00:09:52.170 --> 00:09:54.450
And that gives you the
traditional presence

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:57.270
that you have, but also
gives you another option.

00:09:57.270 --> 00:09:58.980
So between the
three options, that

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:01.650
gives you a lot of
flexibility on designing

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:04.410
your own infrastructure
to meet your needs

00:10:04.410 --> 00:10:07.980
and the appropriate operational
model for your business.

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:12.120
For a sample edge architecture
and how the edge is evolving,

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:14.550
this is, for example, a real
time inventory management

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:16.270
and analytics architecture.

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:18.690
So if you build this
architecture just

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:20.820
on a cloud-only
kind of set up, this

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:22.320
is how it would
look like, where you

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:25.705
have the point of sale inventory
and RFID systems in the store,

00:10:25.705 --> 00:10:27.330
but then you have a
lot of capabilities

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:30.270
in a cloud provider like
Google Cloud Platform,

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:34.140
using technologies like Cloud
Pub/Sub, aggregation analytics,

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:36.750
and video APIs.

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:38.880
But we also have in
this, what Anthos

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:41.700
gives you is a new architecture
where you can move in some

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:43.860
of those capabilities
to in-store,

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:46.200
again, to make it
closer to where you are,

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:48.750
so that you can leverage
your platform of your choice

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:50.670
and the services that
you need, but still

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:52.320
have the latency
and the sensitivity.

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:54.750
So again, flexibility
in the architecture

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:57.000
of how you build
your applications

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:58.590
gives you a lot
of options on how

00:10:58.590 --> 00:11:00.790
you want to run your business.

00:11:00.790 --> 00:11:03.480
So in terms of
innovation, our goal

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:06.960
is to help you with our
industry-specific solutions,

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:09.390
from retail, health care,
financial services, media

00:11:09.390 --> 00:11:11.700
and telco, manufacturer,
and public sectors.

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:14.010
Across all of these,
there are many solutions

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:17.100
that are built out there,
best practices and expertise

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:19.920
within Google Cloud that we
can leverage and help you

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:22.630
to build your own
industry-specific solution.

00:11:22.630 --> 00:11:24.390
So next, I would
like to introduce

00:11:24.390 --> 00:11:27.420
a use case of using Anthos
at the edge with Major League

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:29.333
Baseball.

00:11:29.333 --> 00:11:30.250
KRIS AMY: Thanks, Amr.

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:31.840
Thanks for having me here.

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:34.320
So I thought, what
better way to start

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:36.990
this whole section of the
presentation off than,

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:40.750
let's go through what an actual
end user experience is like.

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:44.070
So what we're seeing here is a
product that we call Game Day.

00:11:44.070 --> 00:11:45.090
You go to MLB.com.

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:46.050
You click on a game.

00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:50.350
You can see a sort of virtual
experience in a game here.

00:11:50.350 --> 00:11:53.130
And what we're seeing
is play by play data.

00:11:53.130 --> 00:11:55.350
We're seeing pitches
and how the pitches were

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:57.570
thrown, what the
velocity was, where

00:11:57.570 --> 00:11:59.480
they were in the strike zone.

00:11:59.480 --> 00:12:02.960
Then we get into what
actual plays happened.

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:04.480
Did a runner steal?

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:05.730
Was there a pick-off attempt?

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:07.330
Was there a home run hit?

00:12:07.330 --> 00:12:12.060
And even that, we're loading
videos of key parts of the game

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:14.690
straight into the feed.

00:12:14.690 --> 00:12:17.450
And so how does this
all come together?

00:12:17.450 --> 00:12:20.800
We start off with our new
stack [? cos ?] tracking system

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:22.960
that we have for 2020.

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:25.480
This is a completely
new system that we've

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:27.220
developed and deployed.

00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:29.860
It uses machine vision
with a whole stack

00:12:29.860 --> 00:12:34.270
of cameras that pick
up velocity, spin

00:12:34.270 --> 00:12:37.740
rate of the ball, where players
are, what their poses are,

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:39.677
and what's happening
in the game.

00:12:39.677 --> 00:12:41.260
So what we're seeing
here is a pitcher

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:42.343
from the New York Yankees.

00:12:42.343 --> 00:12:46.530
This is a video that we took
last year of them actually

00:12:46.530 --> 00:12:47.030
throwing.

00:12:47.030 --> 00:12:48.400
You can see how the ball--

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:49.570
we pick up the trajectory.

00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:52.662
We can put it back in time to
work out where it came from

00:12:52.662 --> 00:12:54.370
and where it's going
to be going through.

00:12:57.460 --> 00:12:59.508
So how does this all work?

00:12:59.508 --> 00:13:01.050
Well, as you know
in baseball, it all

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:02.970
starts with a pitcher
throwing a pitch.

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:04.290
So we kick it off.

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:06.060
We have an on-field
play that happens.

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:08.400
There's someone in the
stadium, all remote, who

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:10.250
clicks for the
start of the play,

00:13:10.250 --> 00:13:13.277
and they complete the
play once it's finished.

00:13:13.277 --> 00:13:15.735
Our tracking system is going
to absorb all the data and all

00:13:15.735 --> 00:13:19.320
the events that are happening
throughout that play,

00:13:19.320 --> 00:13:22.820
we aggregate it, and we process
it in stadium on Anthos.

00:13:22.820 --> 00:13:25.230
So we collect all of
that data, we process it,

00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:28.380
we set up a play, and then we
ship it up to Google Cloud,

00:13:28.380 --> 00:13:31.380
or potentially in stadium if
we have other subscribers that

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:35.040
need that information for
low-latency reasons in stadium.

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:39.517
So where do we come from?

00:13:39.517 --> 00:13:40.100
What do we do?

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:46.150
So in 2019, we had an aging
hardware stack in stadium.

00:13:46.150 --> 00:13:47.400
You can see that on the right.

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:49.410
That's what the end
result looks like.

00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:51.080
We had this aging hardware.

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:55.050
We were bringing a new
tracking solution coming in,

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:59.040
and we needed a way to
simplify, modernize, and bring

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:03.400
consistency to our on-premise
or in-stadium environment

00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:06.330
to what we run in the cloud.

00:14:06.330 --> 00:14:08.010
And we had to have
something that

00:14:08.010 --> 00:14:10.260
would run there, be
stable, and enable

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:12.550
the throughput and the
latency requirements

00:14:12.550 --> 00:14:13.990
that we have in stadium.

00:14:17.880 --> 00:14:20.230
So what does our
environment look like?

00:14:20.230 --> 00:14:22.550
Last year we virtually
had one virtual machine

00:14:22.550 --> 00:14:25.010
for every application
we ran in the stadium.

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:26.870
It had some local
storage attached to it.

00:14:26.870 --> 00:14:30.230
We packaged our apps with RMPs.

00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:32.985
We had no real
automatic failover.

00:14:32.985 --> 00:14:34.610
We had to run two
copies of everything,

00:14:34.610 --> 00:14:36.620
because if we lost
one, we had no way

00:14:36.620 --> 00:14:38.860
to automatically
stand it up and run it

00:14:38.860 --> 00:14:40.933
by a second location
or anything else.

00:14:40.933 --> 00:14:43.130
And if we had a burst
in the stadium where

00:14:43.130 --> 00:14:44.990
we had to process
a whole lot of data

00:14:44.990 --> 00:14:47.450
and potentially we were going
to resend a whole lot of data

00:14:47.450 --> 00:14:49.192
from a game that
happened in the past,

00:14:49.192 --> 00:14:51.150
and we want to get it
processed really quickly,

00:14:51.150 --> 00:14:54.060
we had no way to
flexibly to scale up,

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:55.970
scale down as we needed.

00:14:55.970 --> 00:14:57.720
So we come into 2020.

00:14:57.720 --> 00:14:59.160
What do we do?

00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:00.600
We took Anthos on prem.

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:02.690
We're running on top of VMware.

00:15:02.690 --> 00:15:04.280
We made the decision that--

00:15:04.280 --> 00:15:07.935
we saw Anthos as this
standardization for containers

00:15:07.935 --> 00:15:09.560
for all of our
applications, regardless

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:12.590
of where we ran them, whether
that's in Google Cloud,

00:15:12.590 --> 00:15:15.200
whether that's on prem,
or even in our stadiums.

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:18.500
We had this key requirement
for automatic failover

00:15:18.500 --> 00:15:20.970
for our processing
of all worker nodes,

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:22.470
and we needed out
applications to be

00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:24.902
able to horizontally
scale in the stadium,

00:15:24.902 --> 00:15:26.360
just like they
would scale if we're

00:15:26.360 --> 00:15:27.527
running on GKE in the cloud.

00:15:31.180 --> 00:15:33.080
So our architecture,
it's probably

00:15:33.080 --> 00:15:35.900
a little bit different
from your standard Anthos

00:15:35.900 --> 00:15:37.783
on-prem architecture.

00:15:37.783 --> 00:15:39.200
We have our admin
workstation that

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:42.050
runs inside GCP in the cloud.

00:15:42.050 --> 00:15:44.740
And then for every ballpark,
we've got two here displayed.

00:15:44.740 --> 00:15:46.910
We've got the Rockies
and the Marlins.

00:15:46.910 --> 00:15:49.800
We don't have shared
storage in the park.

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:53.240
We basically have two
processing nodes that we run.

00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:54.830
They run VMware.

00:15:54.830 --> 00:15:57.690
And then on top of that,
they have local storage.

00:15:57.690 --> 00:16:01.110
We run the Seesaw load balancer
setup for each cluster.

00:16:01.110 --> 00:16:02.410
So we had each node.

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:03.612
It's its own cluster.

00:16:03.612 --> 00:16:05.320
It has its own admin
cluster, the control

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:06.600
[? planes, ?] et cetera.

00:16:06.600 --> 00:16:09.705
And then inside that actual
physical hardware machine,

00:16:09.705 --> 00:16:11.580
we had the various worker
nodes that we need.

00:16:11.580 --> 00:16:14.445
And in our case, we run
three different worker nodes.

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:20.690
So what's our end
user interaction?

00:16:20.690 --> 00:16:23.720
We do all this processing
in stadium on Anthos.

00:16:23.720 --> 00:16:25.490
Where does the data go?

00:16:25.490 --> 00:16:27.290
We'll ship it to
in-stadium experiences.

00:16:27.290 --> 00:16:29.723
So that could be up on
the scoreboard you'll see.

00:16:29.723 --> 00:16:31.640
You know you can see
that at the score change,

00:16:31.640 --> 00:16:35.360
or you see a pitch speed, or
what type of pitch it was.

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:38.960
We offload it to our broadcast
partners watching on TV.

00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:42.080
And you see the pitch trajectory
or [? where ?] the home run

00:16:42.080 --> 00:16:44.540
was hit, et cetera.

00:16:44.540 --> 00:16:47.660
We also then take that data, and
we ship it up to Google Cloud,

00:16:47.660 --> 00:16:50.238
where you get experience
from our digital properties

00:16:50.238 --> 00:16:50.780
and products.

00:16:50.780 --> 00:16:53.060
That's what we started
this whole session off with

00:16:53.060 --> 00:16:55.535
was Game Day, which is one
of those digital properties

00:16:55.535 --> 00:16:57.230
or products.

00:16:57.230 --> 00:17:00.410
We also do advanced data
analytics on all this,

00:17:00.410 --> 00:17:02.661
and we do AI and ML
to work out what's

00:17:02.661 --> 00:17:05.119
going to be the new metric that
we're going to see come out

00:17:05.119 --> 00:17:10.770
of baseball in 2020 or 2021?

00:17:10.770 --> 00:17:13.680
So where do we go to from here?

00:17:13.680 --> 00:17:18.300
I covered off just before what
we do right now on the web.

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:21.660
What we got loaded up
here is a rendering

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:25.050
of an actual play that
occurred virtually.

00:17:25.050 --> 00:17:28.470
It's concepts around, what
can we do with this data

00:17:28.470 --> 00:17:30.210
that we haven't ever thought of?

00:17:30.210 --> 00:17:33.600
And this is where we see Anthos
is a key for our go forward

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:36.460
with all of our data that
we're shipping from the park.

00:17:36.460 --> 00:17:39.490
So with that, I'd like
to throw it back to Amr.

00:17:39.490 --> 00:17:41.860
Really great to be
here today, and really

00:17:41.860 --> 00:17:43.710
excited to see what
Anthos can offer.

00:17:43.710 --> 00:17:45.460
AMR ABDELRAZIK: Thank
you very much, Kris.

00:17:45.460 --> 00:17:47.338
That was a very cool use case.

00:17:47.338 --> 00:17:48.880
And as you have
seen, it demonstrated

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:50.950
the power of Anthos.

00:17:50.950 --> 00:17:54.200
MLB team had a virtualized
infrastructure at the edge.

00:17:54.200 --> 00:17:54.970
They deploy it.

00:17:54.970 --> 00:17:57.850
It's the same infrastructure
that they deploy on the cloud,

00:17:57.850 --> 00:18:01.180
and had the same infrastructure
as they migrate to bare metal.

00:18:01.180 --> 00:18:03.520
So our goal with
Anthos is to bring

00:18:03.520 --> 00:18:06.940
the same level of flexibility
and the power of Google Cloud

00:18:06.940 --> 00:18:08.950
to the edge with
an open application

00:18:08.950 --> 00:18:12.980
platform, flexible deployment
option, and a strong ecosystem.

00:18:12.980 --> 00:18:16.990
So I encourage you all
to try Anthos on GCP.

00:18:16.990 --> 00:18:18.450
You can see this link here.

00:18:18.450 --> 00:18:20.800
The link has a lot
of tutorials, a lot

00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:24.650
to demonstrate and try a lot
of the capabilities of Anthos.

00:18:24.650 --> 00:18:27.340
And at the end, I just
want to thank you all

00:18:27.340 --> 00:18:29.230
for giving us the time today.

00:18:29.230 --> 00:18:32.140
We know it's a crazy world out
there, so we really appreciate

00:18:32.140 --> 00:18:34.330
you taking the time to
learn about our technology

00:18:34.330 --> 00:18:36.880
and our product, and we look
forward to partner and work

00:18:36.880 --> 00:18:37.380
with.

00:18:37.380 --> 00:18:38.030
You.

00:18:38.030 --> 00:18:39.660
Thank you.

